// Bounded projection: `T.X` where T is a generic parameter whose bound is an
// interface that requires a readable member X. The projection resolves to X's
// required read type — the bound version of read/projection symmetry. A
// requirement typed `self` resolves to the parameter itself, and the value read
// `v.X` on a value of type T resolves through the same bound, so the type-position
// projection and the value read agree. A type parameter is a compile-time type,
// not a value: it projects in type position (T.X) and reads off a value (v.X), but
// is never consumed as a value itself.

/// A behaviour for anything with a readable level and a readable best of its own
/// type. Both are readable-member requirements (`X: T`, no parentheses), so a
/// field or a getter meets them.
pub interface Levelled {
  /// A readable member: every Levelled is read as `v.level` for its level.
  level: nint

  /// A readable member typed self: read as `v.best`, a value of the same type.
  best: self
}

/// A card meets the level requirement with a stored field and the self-typed best
/// requirement with a getter — the two readable members, one of each kind, the way
/// a type opts into a readable-member bound.
pub type Card = {
  level: nint,
  rank: nint,
} impl Levelled {
  pub get best(): self {
    return self
  }
}

// Type-position projection off the bound: `T.level` is the required read type
// (nint), and `T.best` is self resolved to the parameter (T itself) — the field
// types of a record generic over a bounded parameter are projected off its bound.
/// A summary whose field types are projected off the bounded parameter:
/// `T.level` is nint, `T.best` is T — the requirement typed self resolving to the
/// parameter.
pub type Summary<T: Levelled> = {
  score: T.level,
  top: T.best,
}

// The value half: `v.level` reads the bound's requirement through a value of type
// T, resolving to the same nint the type-position projection `T.level` yields —
// read and projection symmetric over the bound.
/// Read the level off a bounded parameter's value — resolved through the bound,
/// the value twin of the `T.level` projection.
pub fn levelOf<T: Levelled>(v: T): nint {
  return v.level
}
